    body: `<p>The most dangerous thing that has happened to marriage in the modern West is not the sexual revolution. It is not no-fault divorce. It is not the redefinition of the institution in law or culture. The most dangerous thing is that we stopped understanding what kind of thing marriage is. We turned a covenant into a contract, and then we wondered why it kept breaking.</p>

<p>A contract is an exchange of goods and services between two parties who retain their autonomy. If one party fails to deliver, the other is released. A covenant is something else entirely. A covenant is an exchange of selves. It does not say, "I will give you this as long as you give me that." It says, "I am yours. Period. The terms do not change when the feelings do." The entire biblical narrative — from Eden to Sinai to Calvary — runs on covenant. And marriage, in Scripture, is not merely an illustration of that narrative. It is one of its primary theaters.</p>

<p>I have performed more than a hundred weddings in fifteen years of ministry. I have watched couples stand at the altar in the full blaze of romantic certainty, voices steady, eyes locked, utterly convinced that their love is the kind that will not falter. I have sat with some of those same couples five years later in my office, the certainty gone, the voices unsteady, asking whether God would really hold them to promises they made when they did not know what the promises would cost. The answer to that question requires a theology of covenant, and the church has largely failed to provide one.</p>

<h2>The Ancient Near Eastern Background</h2>

<p>To understand biblical covenant, you must understand what covenants meant in the ancient world before Israel adapted the form for its own purposes. In the ancient Near East, a covenant — Hebrew berith — was the most solemn form of agreement two parties could enter. The Hittite suzerainty treaties of the second millennium BCE established a pattern: a powerful king would bind himself to a vassal, not because the vassal had earned the relationship, but because the king chose to extend it. The treaty included a historical prologue recounting what the king had done, stipulations for the vassal's conduct, blessings for faithfulness, curses for unfaithfulness, and a ceremony involving the cutting of animals.</p>

<p>That last detail matters more than it might seem. The Hebrew phrase for making a covenant is karat berith — literally, "to cut a covenant." In Genesis 15, when God makes His covenant with Abraham, the patriarch cuts animals in half and arranges the pieces in two rows with a path between them. In the ancient world, both parties walked between the pieces, symbolically saying, "May I be torn apart like these animals if I break this covenant." But in Genesis 15, something extraordinary happens. God alone — represented as a smoking fire pot and a blazing torch — passes between the pieces. Abraham does not walk. God walks alone. The covenant is unilateral. God binds Himself, absorbs the risk, and stakes His own integrity on the promise.</p>

<p>This is the theological DNA that enters every biblical covenant, including marriage. And the church has largely forgotten it.</p>

<h2>Marriage as Divine Pedagogy</h2>

<p>The prophet Hosea received the strangest ministry assignment in all of Scripture: marry a woman who will be unfaithful to you. Gomer's infidelity was not incidental to Hosea's prophetic work. It was the prophetic work. God used a broken marriage to teach Israel something about divine love that no sermon could have communicated — that the covenant partner does not leave when the other partner wanders. That faithfulness is not contingent on reciprocity. That love, in the biblical sense, is not an emotion that arises in response to the beloved's worthiness but a decision that persists in the face of the beloved's unworthiness.</p>

<p>Jeremiah extended this imagery. Ezekiel made it graphic. By the time Paul writes Ephesians 5, he is working with a metaphorical tradition that has been building for a thousand years: marriage is an icon of the relationship between God and God's people. "This mystery is profound," Paul writes, "and I am saying that it refers to Christ and the church." The profundity is not merely spiritual. It is structural. Marriage, in the biblical imagination, is designed to function as a living parable — a daily, embodied demonstration of what it looks like when two parties bind themselves together in a bond that neither party can unilaterally dissolve.</p>

<p>This is why the stakes are so high. Not because God is a rule-maker who enjoys holding people to uncomfortable commitments. But because marriage is the primary institution through which human beings learn what covenant love feels like from the inside. Every other covenant in Scripture — the Abrahamic, the Mosaic, the Davidic, the New — uses marriage as its interpretive key. If you gut the institution of its covenantal content, you lose the analogy, and with it, the capacity to understand the God who made it.</p>

<h2>What Covenant Is Not</h2>

<p>Before going further, I need to say what covenant marriage is not, because the concept has been badly abused.</p>

<p>Covenant is not a weapon. I have watched men use the theology of covenant to trap their wives in abusive marriages, quoting Malachi 2:16 — "I hate divorce, says the Lord" — while their wives carried bruises that the congregation was not allowed to see. This is not covenant theology. This is coercion dressed in religious language, and it is blasphemous. The God who makes covenants is the God who delivered Israel from Egypt, the God who does not tolerate the oppression of the vulnerable. A theology of covenant that enables abuse has betrayed the very God it claims to honor.</p>

<p>Covenant is not mere endurance. Two people can remain legally married for fifty years while living in emotional exile from each other — two strangers sharing a roof and a tax return, performing the choreography of marriage without inhabiting its substance. This is not faithfulness. It is inertia. The covenant calls not merely for presence but for engagement, not merely for staying but for turning toward the other person again and again, even when every instinct says to turn away.</p>

<p>Covenant is not the absence of cost. The prosperity gospel has a marital counterpart — the assumption that if you do marriage God's way, it will be satisfying, fulfilling, and deeply pleasurable. Sometimes it will be. Sometimes it will be the hardest thing you have ever done, a daily crucifixion of your preferences and your autonomy, a relentless exposure of selfishness you did not know you carried. The covenant does not promise ease. It promises presence. And presence, in a fallen world, is costly.</p>

<h2>The Reformation and the Loss of Sacrament</h2>

<p>Something significant happened to the theology of marriage during the Protestant Reformation, and its effects are still with us. The medieval Catholic Church had classified marriage as one of seven sacraments — a visible sign of invisible grace, an institution that not only symbolized divine reality but participated in it. When Luther and Calvin challenged the sacramental system, they demoted marriage from sacrament to ordinance. They did not do this carelessly. Their concern was legitimate: the sacramental classification had given the church legal jurisdiction over marriage, creating a sprawling canon-law apparatus that was often more about institutional power than pastoral care.</p>

<p>But something was lost in the demotion. When marriage ceased to be a sacrament, it became easier to think of it as merely a social arrangement — a good and godly one, but an arrangement nonetheless. The Reformers themselves did not take this step. Luther called marriage a "worldly thing" but meant by this that it belonged to God's governance of creation, not that it was trivial. Calvin saw marriage as a covenant that mirrored God's covenant with Israel. But their theological heirs, over the centuries, gradually drained the institution of its theological density. By the time the Enlightenment arrived, the groundwork had been laid for marriage to become what it is now in the popular imagination: a contract between two individuals pursuing mutual happiness.</p>

<p>John Witte Jr., in his exhaustive study "From Sacrament to Contract," traced this trajectory with devastating precision. The movement from sacrament to contract was not a single event but a slow erosion, spanning five centuries, in which the theological content of marriage was progressively replaced by legal and therapeutic categories. We did not wake up one morning in a culture that treats marriage as disposable. We arrived here by a long road of theological withdrawal.</p>

<h2>What Covenant Marriage Demands</h2>

<p>If marriage is a covenant rather than a contract, what does that demand of the people inside it? Three things, at minimum.</p>

<p>First, it demands that you stop keeping score. A contract lives by the ledger — I gave this, you owe that. A covenant burns the ledger. This does not mean that imbalances do not matter. It means that the response to imbalance is not withdrawal but confrontation and grace — speaking the truth in love, as Paul instructs in Ephesians 4, and then refusing to hold the debt. The moment you begin tracking what your spouse owes you, you have exited the covenant and entered the marketplace.</p>

<p>Second, it demands that you tell the truth. Covenant relationships cannot survive on pretense. The entire prophetic tradition is God telling Israel the truth about itself — not to destroy but to heal. "Faithful are the wounds of a friend," Proverbs 27:6 says. In marriage, this means that real love will sometimes require you to say the thing your spouse does not want to hear, and it will require you to hear the thing you do not want said. Both are acts of covenant faithfulness. Silence, in a covenant, is not peace. It is abandonment.</p>

<p>Third, it demands that you die. This is the part no one puts on the wedding invitation. The cruciform shape of marriage — the pattern Paul describes in Ephesians 5 when he tells husbands to love their wives as Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her — is not a metaphor for mild self-sacrifice. It is a description of death. The death of your right to be right. The death of your expectation that marriage exists to make you happy. The death of the self that entered the covenant imagining it could be kept at manageable cost.</p>

<p>I have died these deaths. Imperfectly, incompletely, with more resistance than I want to admit. I have stood in my own kitchen at eleven o'clock at night, exhausted, arguing about something that will not matter in a week, and felt every fiber of my being resist the act of laying down my position. The covenant does not care about my fibers. It calls me to die. And in the dying — not always, not immediately, but eventually — something comes to life that could not have existed any other way.</p>

<h2>The Promise and Its Cost</h2>

<p>The deepest paradox of covenant marriage is that its constraints produce the very freedom they appear to deny. The commitment that forecloses other options is the same commitment that creates the safety necessary for genuine intimacy. You cannot be fully known by someone who might leave. You cannot be fully vulnerable with someone whose presence is contingent on your performance. The covenant — the unconditional, irrevocable, until-death commitment — is the only container strong enough to hold the full weight of two human lives pressing against each other.</p>

<p>Stanley Hauerwas, the Methodist ethicist at Duke, put it with his characteristic bluntness: "We always marry the wrong person." His point was not cynicism. It was that the person you marry will inevitably turn out to be different from the person you thought you were marrying, and the person you are will inevitably turn out to be different from the person your spouse thought they were marrying. The question is not whether you married the right person. The question is whether you will keep your covenant with the actual person who shows up — the one with the real flaws, the real struggles, the real capacity to wound you in ways you did not know you could be wounded.</p>

<p>The covenant says yes. Not because the feelings persist. Not because the cost is manageable. Not because the alternative is worse. The covenant says yes because the God who made the covenant is a God who says yes — who walked between the pieces alone, who absorbed the curse, who kept the promise when every rational calculation said to walk away.</p>

<blockquote>We do not stay married because marriage is easy. We do not stay married because we are strong. We stay married because we made a promise in the presence of a God who keeps His promises, and we have staked our lives on the wager that His pattern — not ours — is the one worth following.</blockquote>

<p>That wager will cost you everything. It is also the only path to the kind of love that the contract cannot produce — the love that has been through the fire and came out not unburned but refined. The love that knows what it has survived. The love that, precisely because it was never contingent on feeling, outlasts every feeling and becomes something the world cannot explain.</p>

<p>The culture offers you a contract. The gospel offers you a covenant. The contract is safer. The covenant is better. And the distance between the two is the distance between a transaction and a life.</p>`
