    body: `<p>There is a lie circulating in the church about marriage, and it has done more damage than most heresies. The lie is this: good marriages do not fight. Godly couples resolve their differences calmly, with mutual submission and soft voices, and if you find yourself shouting in the kitchen at ten o'clock at night, something has gone wrong with your sanctification.</p>

<p>This is not wisdom. It is denial dressed in theological clothing. And the couples who believe it either suppress their conflict until it metastasizes into something far worse, or they conclude that their marriage must be uniquely broken because the calm resolution never seems to arrive. Both outcomes are destructive. Both are unnecessary.</p>

<p>The biblical witness tells a different story. The God of Scripture is not conflict-averse. He argues with Abraham over Sodom. He wrestles Jacob at the Jabbok until the man's hip is dislocated. He spends the entire book of Job in a confrontation that leaves both parties — God and the human being — more fully revealed. The prophets are, by definition, agents of conflict, sent to say the things no one wants to hear because the things no one wants to hear are the things everyone needs to hear. Conflict, in the biblical imagination, is not a sign of failure. It is one of the primary instruments through which truth is surfaced and transformation becomes possible.</p>

<p>Marriage is no exception. If anything, marriage is the crucible where this principle is most fiercely tested, because nowhere else are two human beings pressed so closely together for so long with so little room to hide.</p>

<h2>Why We Fight</h2>

<p>The surface reasons for marital conflict are nearly universal and boringly predictable. Money. Sex. Parenting. In-laws. Housework. The distribution of labor, both visible and invisible. These are the topics that fill the minutes of every marital argument, and they matter — but they are almost never the real issue.</p>

<p>Beneath the surface content of every fight lies a deeper question, and the deeper question is almost always about identity, value, or security. "You spent how much?" is rarely about the dollar amount. It is about control, about trust, about the fear that the other person's judgment cannot be relied upon. "You never initiate" is rarely about sex. It is about desirability, about whether you are wanted, about whether the person who promised to choose you is still choosing you. "You always side with your mother" is rarely about the mother. It is about loyalty, about whether the covenant has actually rearranged the hierarchy of allegiances.</p>

<p>James 4:1-2 asks the question with characteristic bluntness: "What causes quarrels and what causes fights among you? Is it not this, that your passions are at war within you? You desire and do not have, so you murder." James is not speaking of literal murder. He is speaking of the violence that erupts when desire goes unmet — the violence of the tongue, the violence of withdrawal, the violence of contempt. The war in the marriage is a reflection of the war within the self. And the self at war with itself will make war with anyone who gets close enough to trigger the conflict.</p>

<p>This is why mere communication technique is insufficient. You can teach a couple to use I-statements and active listening, and they will deploy those techniques with precision while their eyes communicate contempt. The problem is not the vocabulary. The problem is the heart. And the heart, as Jeremiah observed, is deceitful above all things and desperately sick. Who can understand it?</p>

<h2>The Spiritual Discipline of Listening</h2>

<p>The first discipline of fighting well is the discipline of listening — not the performative listening of the communication seminar, where you repeat back what your spouse said to prove you were paying attention, but the genuine listening that requires you to temporarily suspend your own narrative and inhabit your spouse's.</p>

<p>This is extraordinarily difficult, and the difficulty is spiritual, not merely psychological. To genuinely listen to another person's grievance against you is to accept that you might be wrong. Not partially wrong, in the way that allows you to maintain your essential rightness while conceding a minor point. Actually wrong. Wrong in a way that requires repentance, not adjustment. Wrong in a way that reveals something about yourself you did not want to see.</p>

<p>Dietrich Bonhoeffer wrote in "Life Together" that the first service one owes to others in community is the service of listening. "Many people are looking for an ear that will listen," he wrote. "They do not find it among Christians, because these Christians are talking where they should be listening." That observation, made in the context of Christian community, applies with doubled force in marriage. The spouse who listens — who puts down the defense, who stops formulating the rebuttal, who actually receives what the other person is saying — is performing an act of radical self-denial. It is a small death. And, like every death in the Christian life, it is the doorway to something that cannot be reached any other way.</p>

<p>I fail at this constantly. In the middle of a disagreement, my mind is not listening. My mind is lawyering — building the case, cataloging the precedents, preparing the closing argument. I am so busy being right that I cannot hear the person I love telling me that she is in pain. And the rightness, even when it is factually accurate, becomes a kind of cruelty, because it prioritizes being correct over being present. The cross has something to say about that priority, and what it says is not comfortable.</p>

<h2>The Discipline of Honest Speech</h2>

<p>The second discipline is the discipline of honest speech — saying what is true rather than what is safe. This cuts against the grain of most conflict-avoidant marriages, where peace is maintained by the mutual agreement never to name the thing that both parties know is there.</p>

<p>Ephesians 4:15 instructs believers to speak the truth in love. Both halves of that phrase are essential and both are difficult. Truth without love is brutality — the spouse who uses honesty as a weapon, who says, "I am just being honest" while delivering a wound that will take years to heal. Love without truth is sentimentality — the spouse who says everything is fine when everything is collapsing, who mistakes niceness for kindness and avoidance for peace.</p>

<p>Honest speech in a marriage means naming the pattern that both of you have been dancing around for months. It means saying, "When you dismiss my opinion in front of our friends, I feel humiliated," instead of withdrawing into a cold silence that your spouse must somehow decode. It means saying, "I am afraid that we are growing apart" instead of burying the fear under busyness and hoping it resolves itself. It means, sometimes, saying, "I am angry with you, and the anger is legitimate, and I need you to hear it before I can let it go."</p>

<p>This kind of speech requires courage, and the courage is spiritual. The reason most people avoid honest speech in marriage is not that they lack the vocabulary. It is that honest speech creates vulnerability, and vulnerability creates the possibility of rejection. To say "this hurt me" is to reveal that you can be hurt, and to reveal that you can be hurt is to hand the other person a power you cannot control. The entire fallen human project is a project of self-protection — covering ourselves, hiding our nakedness, building walls between our vulnerability and the world. Honest speech in marriage dismantles those walls, and the dismantling feels like dying because, in a very real sense, it is.</p>

<h2>The Discipline of Repair</h2>

<p>The third discipline is the discipline of repair — the willingness to return to the breach after the fight, to acknowledge what was said and done, and to rebuild what was damaged. This is where most couples fail, not because they lack the will but because they lack the model.</p>

<p>The cultural models for post-conflict behavior are uniformly inadequate. The "move on" model says: the fight is over, let it go, stop bringing it up. This model produces couples who accumulate unprocessed wounds like geological strata, each layer hardening over time until the marriage is built on a foundation of unresolved pain. The "process everything" model says: we must talk about every conflict until both parties feel fully heard and completely resolved. This model produces couples who spend so much time processing their marriage that they have no energy left to live it.</p>

<p>The biblical model is different. It involves confession, forgiveness, and what I would call covenantal re-commitment — the explicit reaffirmation, after the damage, that the covenant still holds. "I was wrong. I am sorry. I am still here." Those three sentences, spoken honestly after a genuine conflict, contain more theological content than most sermons. They enact, in miniature, the entire gospel: the acknowledgment of sin, the appeal for grace, the persistence of covenant love in the face of human failure.</p>

<p>The discipline of repair also requires the willingness to forgive — genuinely, not performatively. I will write about forgiveness at length elsewhere, but here I will say this: forgiveness in marriage is not a feeling. It is a decision to cancel the debt, to refuse to use the offense as leverage in future conflicts, to treat the person who wounded you as though the wound has been healed even when it still hurts. This is unjust by every human standard of fairness. It is also the only thing that works, because the alternative — keeping the ledger, hoarding the grievances, building the case — turns the marriage into a courtroom, and no one has ever been loved in a courtroom.</p>

<h2>What Conflict Reveals</h2>

<p>If you will let it, conflict in marriage will teach you things about yourself that no other experience can reveal. It will show you how quickly you retreat to self-protection. It will show you the particular words and tones that trigger your oldest wounds. It will show you the gap between who you believe yourself to be and who you actually are at eleven o'clock at night when you are tired and your defenses are down.</p>

<p>This is sanctification. Not the clean, upward-trending sanctification of the systematic theology textbook, where the believer progresses steadily from glory to glory. The actual sanctification of the New Testament, which involves being conformed to the image of Christ through suffering, through failure, through the repeated death of the self that refuses to die. Marriage is the most relentless instrument of this sanctification, because marriage does not let you hide. You can perform competence at work. You can perform godliness at church. You cannot perform anything at home, because the person who shares your bed knows exactly who you are when the performance ends.</p>

<p>Gary Thomas asked the question that reframed the entire conversation: "What if God designed marriage to make us holy more than to make us happy?" The question is not a denial of happiness. It is a reordering of priorities. Happiness is the byproduct, not the goal. Holiness — the slow, painful, beautiful process of becoming the person God made you to be — is the goal. And conflict is one of the primary means by which that goal is pursued.</p>

<blockquote>The couples who fight well are not the couples who fight less. They are the couples who have learned to stay in the room when every instinct says to leave, to keep their hands open when every instinct says to make a fist, to return to each other after the damage and say the three hardest sentences in any language: I was wrong. I am sorry. I am still here.</blockquote>

<p>The fight is not the enemy of the marriage. The refusal to fight honestly — or the refusal to repair after the fight — is the enemy. And the church that teaches its couples to avoid conflict is teaching them to avoid the very furnace in which their love might be refined into something that lasts.</p>

<p>The furnace is hot. It is supposed to be. The gold does not come out any other way.</p>`
