    body: `<p>It does not happen all at once. It happens over months, sometimes years. One spouse picks up a book and something shifts. One spouse starts therapy and begins to see patterns that were invisible before. One spouse has a crisis — a death, a diagnosis, a moment of clarity in the shower — and the world rearranges itself around a new center of gravity. The growth is genuine. It is good. And it creates a problem that no one warned them about.</p>

<p>Because growth in a marriage is not symmetrical. It does not happen at the same pace, in the same direction, or in response to the same catalysts. One person wakes up. The other is still sleeping. And the distance between them, which was always there in some form, becomes visible in a way that can no longer be ignored.</p>

<p>I have watched this happen in my congregation more times than I can count. I have watched it happen in my own marriage. And I have learned that the church's typical responses — "be patient," "pray harder," "submit to one another" — are not wrong, exactly, but they are insufficient. They do not address the specific loneliness of being married to someone who does not seem to want what you now want, does not see what you now see, and cannot understand why you are no longer content with what used to be enough.</p>

<h2>The Grief of Asymmetric Growth</h2>

<p>The first thing that needs to be named is the grief. When one spouse grows and the other does not, the growing spouse grieves — not for something lost, but for something never arrived. They grieve the conversations they want to have but cannot. They grieve the emotional depth they have discovered but cannot share. They grieve the version of the marriage they can now imagine but cannot build alone.</p>

<p>This grief is compounded by guilt, because the growing spouse usually knows, at some level, that their growth has made things harder. They have changed the terms of the relationship without the other person's consent. The marriage that was working — or at least functioning — under the old terms no longer works under the new ones. And the growing spouse feels responsible for the disruption, even as they know they could not stop the growth if they tried.</p>

<p>The non-growing spouse has their own grief, though they may not name it as such. They experience the other's growth as a kind of abandonment — a departure from the shared world they thought they inhabited together. "You have changed," they say, and what they mean is, "You have left me." The accusation is not entirely unfair. Growth, when it is genuine, does create distance. The person who has seen something new cannot pretend they have not seen it, and the person who has not seen it cannot understand why the pretending has stopped.</p>

<p>I have been on both sides of this dynamic. I have been the spouse who was growing, frustrated by what felt like resistance and stagnation. And I have been the spouse who was not growing — or not growing in that particular direction — and experienced my partner's growth as an implicit criticism of who I was. Both positions are painful. Neither is simple. And the worst thing the church can do is flatten the complexity into a formula.</p>

<h2>The Theology of Uneven Sanctification</h2>

<p>The Reformed tradition speaks of sanctification as a process — the gradual work of the Holy Spirit conforming the believer to the image of Christ. What the tradition does not adequately address is the fact that this process is wildly uneven, not only between individuals but within individuals. You may grow rapidly in patience and not at all in honesty. You may develop a rich prayer life while your capacity for emotional presence remains stunted. Sanctification is not a uniform upgrade. It is a jagged, selective, unpredictable renovation.</p>

<p>In marriage, this unevenness becomes impossible to ignore. You are living with someone whose renovation is on a different schedule than yours, targeting different rooms, using different blueprints. And because you share the same house, every change one person makes affects the other, whether the other is ready for the change or not.</p>

<p>Paul addresses this obliquely in 1 Corinthians 7, when he speaks about the believing spouse married to the unbelieving spouse. His counsel is striking in its restraint: stay married, because "how do you know, wife, whether you will save your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will save your wife?" The theology underneath that counsel is a theology of patience that does not promise results. Paul does not say, "Stay, and your spouse will eventually come around." He says, "Stay, and you do not know what will happen." The uncertainty is the point. Faith, by definition, operates in the absence of guarantees.</p>

<p>But Paul's situation — a believer married to an unbeliever — is actually simpler than the situation I am describing. What happens when both spouses are believers, both claim to follow Christ, both attend church and read Scripture, but one is being transformed and the other is merely performing? What happens when the gap is not between faith and unbelief but between depth and surface, between a faith that costs and a faith that comforts?</p>

<h2>The Temptation of Contempt</h2>

<p>Here is the danger that the growing spouse must face: the temptation to feel superior. Growth, when it is genuine, produces humility — a deeper awareness of one's own brokenness, a greater capacity for compassion, a reduced need to be right. But growth, when it is incomplete, can produce the opposite — a spiritual arrogance that looks down on the spouse who has not yet arrived where you have arrived.</p>

<p>This arrogance is devastating to a marriage. It communicates, in every tone and glance and sigh, that the other person is not enough. That they are behind. That you have outgrown them. And the cruelest form of this arrogance is the one that wraps itself in spiritual language — "I just wish you were more intentional," "I need someone who can meet me at this level," "I am growing and you are staying the same." These sentences, spoken from a position of spiritual superiority, are not expressions of love. They are expressions of contempt, and contempt — as I have written elsewhere — is the most corrosive force in any relationship.</p>

<p>Jesus told a parable about two men who went up to the temple to pray. One was a Pharisee, the other a tax collector. The Pharisee thanked God that he was not like other people. The tax collector beat his breast and said, "God, be merciful to me, a sinner." Jesus said the tax collector went home justified. The parable is a warning to everyone who has ever stood in the temple of their own growth and looked down at the person who has not grown as fast.</p>

<p>If your growth has made you kind, patient, and more capable of loving the person you married, it is genuine growth. If your growth has made you critical, impatient, and convinced that you deserve a more evolved partner, it is not growth at all. It is pride with a new vocabulary.</p>

<h2>What the Non-Growing Spouse Might Be Carrying</h2>

<p>Before I go further, I need to say something in defense of the spouse who appears to be standing still. Because the appearance of non-growth is not always what it seems.</p>

<p>Some people do not grow because they are resistant — because growth threatens the identity they have constructed, because change is terrifying, because the familiar dysfunction is preferable to the unfamiliar health. This is real, and it needs to be named.</p>

<p>But some people do not grow because they are exhausted. They are carrying burdens the growing spouse cannot see — depression that has not been diagnosed, anxiety that manifests as rigidity, trauma that has been buried so deep it does not register as trauma but as personality. The spouse who seems unwilling to grow may be unable to grow — not because they lack the desire but because their internal resources are fully committed to survival.</p>

<p>Others are growing in ways that are invisible to their partner. The spouse who cannot articulate emotional insight may be growing in faithfulness, in consistency, in the quiet discipline of showing up every day and doing the work without fanfare. Growth does not always look like self-awareness. Sometimes it looks like endurance. And the marriage that dismisses endurance as stagnation has confused depth with articulateness.</p>

<p>I have watched husbands sit in my office, unable to name a single emotion, dismissed by their wives as emotionally unavailable, while those same husbands worked seventy-hour weeks without complaint to provide for families who never thanked them. That is not nothing. That is a form of love that does not translate well into the language of emotional intelligence, but it is love nonetheless. The growing spouse who cannot see this love is not as perceptive as they think.</p>

<h2>The Path Forward</h2>

<p>There is no formula for navigating asymmetric growth in a marriage. But there are disciplines that can keep the asymmetry from becoming a permanent division.</p>

<p>The first discipline is honesty without superiority. The growing spouse must be honest about what they need and what they see, but the honesty must be delivered from a position of solidarity, not from a height. "I am struggling with where we are" is different from "You are not enough for me." The first is an invitation. The second is a verdict.</p>

<p>The second discipline is curiosity without agenda. Before assuming that your spouse is not growing, ask them what is happening inside them. Ask what they are struggling with. Ask what they are afraid of. And listen — not to fix, not to diagnose, not to assess whether their answer meets your standard of growth, but to understand. You may discover that the person you thought was standing still has been running a marathon you could not see.</p>

<p>The third discipline is patience without passivity. Patience does not mean accepting a permanent status quo without comment. It means accepting that transformation takes time and that you do not control the timeline. You can invite. You can model. You can create conditions that make growth possible. You cannot make another person grow. That is the work of the Spirit, and the Spirit does not operate on your schedule.</p>

<p>The fourth discipline is the hardest: the willingness to grieve what is and to love what is at the same time. To grieve the marriage you imagined while investing fully in the marriage you have. To hold the tension between what you want and what exists, without resolving it prematurely in either direction — neither pretending that the gap does not matter nor concluding that the gap cannot be crossed.</p>

<blockquote>The marriage where one spouse grows and the other does not is not a failed marriage. It is a marriage in the middle of its story — a story whose ending has not been written, whose next chapter may surprise both of them, whose Author has a longer patience than either of them can imagine.</blockquote>

<p>Stay in the story. Not because staying is easy. Not because staying guarantees the outcome you want. Stay because the covenant you made was not contingent on synchronized growth, and the God who holds you both is doing work in both of you that neither of you can fully see. The view from inside the process is always incomplete. The view from the end — the end you have not yet reached — may reveal a coherence that the middle obscured.</p>

<p>That is not a promise. It is a hope. And hope, in the Christian life, is not wishful thinking. It is the substance of things not yet seen, held together by a God who finishes what He starts.</p>`
