    body: `<p>The church has a sex problem, and the problem is not what most people think. It is not that the church talks about sex too much or too little. It is that the church talks about sex badly — vacillating between two equally distorted positions. The first position treats sex as danger, something to be fenced off, warned against, and guarded from with purity pledges and metaphors about chewed gum and used tape. The second position, the evangelical reaction to the first, treats sex as reward — God's gift to married people, the prize for doing it right, the holy pleasure that makes all the waiting worthwhile. Both positions reduce sex to something it is not. Neither arrives at what it is.</p>

<p>What sex is, in the biblical framework, is theology. Not a metaphor for theology. Not an illustration of theological principles. An actual theological act — a bodily enactment of the covenant, a physical participation in the mystery that Paul says is "profound," a revelation of something about God and humanity that cannot be accessed any other way. The church that cannot say this clearly — that cannot hold together the goodness, the gravity, and the vulnerability of sexual intimacy without collapsing into either prudishness or banality — has failed at one of its most fundamental pastoral tasks.</p>

<p>I write this as a pastor who has sat with hundreds of couples whose sexual lives are sources of pain, shame, confusion, and grief. I write this as a man who grew up in a culture that saturated him with sexual imagery and a church that gave him almost nothing to counter it except rules. I write this knowing that the subject requires more honesty than the church typically permits and more reverence than the culture typically offers. Both are necessary. Neither alone is sufficient.</p>

<h2>The Body Is Not the Problem</h2>

<p>The deepest distortion in the church's theology of sex is the assumption, rarely stated but pervasively felt, that the body is the problem. That the sexual urge is a concession to fallen nature rather than a feature of created goodness. That the spiritual life is the real life and the bodily life is the obstacle to it.</p>

<p>This assumption does not come from the Bible. It comes from Plato, filtered through Gnosticism, absorbed by certain strands of early Christianity, and embedded so deeply in Western religious consciousness that most Christians do not realize they hold it. The Gnostic heresy taught that the material world was created by a lesser deity, that the body was a prison for the soul, and that salvation consisted in escaping the body's demands. The early church condemned Gnosticism formally. It absorbed Gnosticism practically. And the practical absorption shows up most clearly in the church's treatment of sex.</p>

<p>The biblical witness is unambiguous. "God saw everything that he had made, and behold, it was very good" (Genesis 1:31). Everything. The bodies. The hormones. The desire. The capacity for physical pleasure. All of it preceded the fall. All of it was pronounced good before sin entered the picture. The Song of Solomon — an entire book of erotic poetry that the church has spent centuries trying to allegorize into something safer — celebrates the beauty of the human body and the pleasure of sexual desire with an explicitness that would make most church small groups deeply uncomfortable.</p>

<p>"Your lips drip nectar, my bride; honey and milk are under your tongue" (Song 4:11). "Let my beloved come to his garden, and eat its choicest fruits" (Song 4:16). "I came to my garden, my sister, my bride, I gathered my myrrh with my spice, I ate my honeycomb with my honey" (Song 5:1). The language is sensual. It is deliberate. And it is Scripture. The God who inspired these words is not embarrassed by the body. The question is why we are.</p>

<h2>The One-Flesh Union Revisited</h2>

<p>I have written elsewhere about the meaning of "one flesh" in Hebrew, and I will not repeat that analysis in full here. But the sexual dimension of the one-flesh union requires specific attention, because it is the dimension the church most consistently mishandles.</p>

<p>When two bodies come together in sexual union, something happens that is not merely biological. The act of sexual intercourse is the most complete physical vulnerability two human beings can share — the removal of every barrier, the exposure of every surface, the mutual surrender of bodily autonomy. It is the bodily enactment of what the covenant promises verbally: I am yours. All of me. Without reservation. Without defense.</p>

<p>This is why sexual intimacy outside of covenant — outside the binding, permanent, public commitment — is, in the biblical framework, not merely rule-breaking but self-contradictory. The body is saying what the will has not said. The act of total physical surrender is being performed without the context of total personal commitment. The flesh is becoming one while the lives remain separate. And the resulting dissonance — the gap between what the body says and what the life says — is what the biblical writers call porneia, sexual immorality. Not because the body is bad. Because the body is saying something that is not true.</p>

<p>Within the covenant, sexual intimacy becomes something else entirely. It becomes a sacramental act — a visible, physical sign of an invisible, spiritual reality. The mutual self-giving of two bodies mirrors the mutual self-giving of two lives. The pleasure is not the point, though it is genuinely good. The vulnerability is the point. The willingness to be fully exposed, fully received, fully held by another person who has promised not to leave — this is what makes marital sex a theological act rather than merely a physical one.</p>

<h2>The Damage Done by Purity Culture</h2>

<p>I cannot write about sexual intimacy in marriage without addressing the wreckage left by the purity culture movement of the 1990s and 2000s. The movement — epitomized by Joshua Harris's "I Kissed Dating Goodbye" (1997), the True Love Waits campaign, and a thousand youth group talks featuring crumpled roses and unwrapped candy bars — was built on a legitimate concern: young people were being harmed by the sexual chaos of the culture, and the church wanted to protect them.</p>

<p>But the protection was purchased at a catastrophic price. Purity culture taught, implicitly and often explicitly, that sexual desire was the enemy — that the body's longing for intimacy was something to be suppressed, controlled, and feared. It taught women that their bodies were dangerous — sources of temptation that must be hidden, covered, and guarded lest they cause a brother to stumble. It taught men that their desire was predatory — an animal impulse that, if not caged by accountability partners and internet filters, would destroy everything it touched.</p>

<p>The result was a generation of Christians who arrived at their wedding nights carrying not anticipation but terror. Sheila Wray Gregoire's research, published in "The Great Sex Rescue," documented what many pastors had observed anecdotally: women who had absorbed purity culture messaging reported significantly higher rates of sexual pain, difficulty with arousal, and inability to experience pleasure in marriage. The message that sex was dangerous before the wedding did not magically transform into the message that sex was beautiful after it. The body does not work that way. The shame that was learned in adolescence did not evaporate with the exchange of rings. It followed them into the marriage bed and took up permanent residence.</p>

<p>The church owes these women — and these men — a profound apology. Not merely for the excesses of the movement but for the underlying theology that made the excesses possible: the theology that treats the body as the enemy of the soul, that confuses purity with repression, that cannot distinguish between sexual discipline and sexual fear.</p>

<h2>What Healthy Sexual Theology Requires</h2>

<p>A theology of sexual intimacy adequate to the biblical witness must hold together at least four things that the church has historically separated.</p>

<p>First, it must hold together goodness and gravity. Sex is good — genuinely, bodily, pleasurably good. It is also weighty — a covenantal act with the power to bond, to heal, to wound, and to reveal. A theology that has only goodness produces triviality. A theology that has only gravity produces fear. The biblical witness holds both: the Song of Solomon's delight and Paul's warning that the body is a temple. The pleasure and the weight belong together. Separating them distorts both.</p>

<p>Second, it must hold together desire and discipline. Sexual desire is not the enemy. It is a created capacity, a gift of the God who made bodies and pronounced them good. But desire, like every created capacity, requires direction. The discipline is not the suppression of desire but the training of desire — learning to want what is good, in the way that is good, at the time that is good. This is true of every appetite. The solution to gluttony is not the elimination of hunger. It is the ordering of hunger toward its proper end. The same is true of sexual desire.</p>

<p>Third, it must hold together mutuality and difference. Paul's instructions in 1 Corinthians 7 are striking in their symmetry: "The husband should give to his wife her conjugal rights, and likewise the wife to her husband. For the wife does not have authority over her own body, but the husband does. Likewise the husband does not have authority over his own body, but the wife does." The symmetry is deliberate and radical. In a first-century context where a wife's body was legally the property of her husband, Paul insists that the authority is mutual. Both bodies belong to the other. Both partners have the right to give and the obligation to receive. Neither can claim ownership without reciprocating it.</p>

<p>This mutuality has been badly abused. The passage has been used to coerce unwilling spouses into sex — "your body is not your own" deployed as a weapon rather than an invitation. This is a perversion of Paul's meaning so profound that it constitutes its own form of sexual sin. The mutuality Paul describes is not about obligation. It is about generosity — the willingness of two people to give themselves to each other freely, without coercion, without manipulation, and without the withholding of the self as punishment.</p>

<p>Fourth, and most difficult, it must hold together the sexual and the sacred without making them identical. Sex is not worship. It is not a substitute for prayer. It does not earn spiritual credit. But it participates in something sacred — the covenant between two people that mirrors the covenant between God and humanity. The church that cannot make this distinction will either sacralize sex into something oppressively holy or desacralize it into something casually biological. Both errors miss the point.</p>

<h2>The Practical Realities</h2>

<p>Theology that does not touch the ground is not theology. It is abstraction. And the ground where sexual intimacy lives in most marriages is more complicated than any theological framework can fully address.</p>

<p>There are couples in every congregation dealing with mismatched desire — one partner who wants sex frequently and another who does not. There are couples dealing with sexual dysfunction — pain, inability to achieve arousal or orgasm, erectile difficulty — that makes the physical act of union difficult or impossible. There are couples carrying wounds from sexual abuse — one in four women and one in six men, according to the CDC — whose bodies respond to sexual touch with fear rather than pleasure. There are couples who have never had an honest conversation about what they enjoy, what they need, and what they do not want, because the church gave them a theology of sex that was too embarrassed to get specific.</p>

<p>These realities require more than theology. They require clinical competence, professional counseling, medical intervention, and the kind of pastoral care that is willing to sit in uncomfortable specificity rather than retreating to comfortable abstraction. The pastor who cannot refer a couple to a sex therapist without embarrassment is not protecting the sanctity of the subject. That pastor is failing the people who need help.</p>

<blockquote>The body is not the enemy of the soul. It is the place where the soul lives. And the sexual union of two people within the covenant of marriage is not a concession to human weakness. It is a participation in divine self-giving — the bodily enactment of a love that holds nothing back, that refuses to hide, and that trusts the other with the whole unguarded self.</blockquote>

<p>The church that can say this — can say it without blushing, without hedging, without retreating into either prudishness or crudeness — will be a church that finally gives its people what they need: not rules about sex, but a theology of sex. A theology that honors the body, respects the covenant, takes pleasure seriously, and refuses to let shame have the last word about something God called very good.</p>

<p>That theology is overdue. The people in our pews have been waiting for it. Some of them have stopped waiting. The church that speaks last speaks to an empty room.</p>`
