    body: `<p>I need to say something that most pastors will not say publicly, because saying it publicly feels like a confession of weakness, and weakness in ministry is the one thing you are never allowed to show. Here it is: there have been seasons in my ministry when my marriage survived not because of my faithfulness but in spite of my unfaithfulness — not unfaithfulness in the sexual sense, but in the vocational sense. I gave the church my best energy, my best attention, my best emotional presence, and I gave my wife what was left. What was left was not enough. It was never enough.</p>

<p>This is not a unique confession. It is a nearly universal one among pastors, and the fact that it is rarely spoken does not mean it is rarely felt. The Barna Group's 2017 study on pastoral health found that 52 percent of pastors reported that the demands of ministry had a negative effect on their family. The Hartford Institute's research found that 80 percent of pastors believed pastoral ministry negatively affected their families. Francis Schaeffer, reflecting on his own ministry failures, wrote with painful honesty about the gap between the community he preached and the marriage he practiced.</p>

<p>The question is not whether ministry is hard on marriages. The question is why. And the answer is more structural than personal, more systemic than spiritual, more about the way we have designed the pastoral role than about the character of the people who fill it.</p>

<h2>The Structural Problem</h2>

<p>The modern pastoral role is structurally designed to destroy marriages. This is not an exaggeration. Consider what the role demands: the pastor is expected to be available at all hours for emergencies — hospital visits, suicidal parishioners, marital crises, deaths. The pastor is expected to prepare and deliver multiple sermons per week, each one representing hours of study, writing, and emotional labor. The pastor is expected to attend committee meetings, lead staff, manage budgets, cast vision, respond to criticism, navigate conflict, visit the elderly, counsel the struggling, and show up at every church event with a smile that communicates both warmth and pastoral authority.</p>

<p>The pastor is also expected to have a thriving marriage and well-adjusted children, because these are understood to be qualifications for the role. First Timothy 3:4-5 says that an overseer must "manage his own household well." The congregation reads this as a requirement and then builds a system that makes compliance nearly impossible. We demand the fruit while poisoning the soil.</p>

<p>The structural problem is compounded by the absence of boundaries that would be considered non-negotiable in any other profession. A surgeon does not take calls during surgery. A trial lawyer does not step out of the courtroom to handle a church member's anxiety attack. But the pastor is expected to leave the dinner table when the phone rings, to cancel the anniversary date when a deacon has a crisis, to prioritize the congregation's needs over the family's needs as a demonstration of ministerial faithfulness.</p>

<p>And the spouse — the pastor's spouse — is expected to accept this arrangement without complaint, because the work is "the Lord's work," and who would compete with the Lord? The theological framing of ministry makes it nearly impossible for the spouse to name the deprivation without feeling guilty. How do you say, "I need you to be home tonight" when the alternative is a teenager in the ER? How do you say, "I need your attention" when the alternative is a grieving widow? The pastor's spouse learns, over time, that their needs will always lose the triage, because the congregation's needs are always more urgent, more visible, and more spiritually weighted.</p>

<h2>The Emotional Depletion</h2>

<p>The structural demands would be manageable if the emotional toll were acknowledged and addressed. It is not. Ministry is one of the most emotionally intensive professions in existence, and it operates with almost no emotional infrastructure.</p>

<p>Consider what a typical week contains. Monday: debriefing from Sunday, responding to the person offended by the sermon, processing the conversation in the lobby with the couple whose marriage is ending. Tuesday: hospital visit with a dying church member, followed by a staff meeting about budget shortfalls, followed by a counseling session with a man struggling with pornography. Wednesday: midweek teaching, youth group crisis, email from a board member questioning a decision. Thursday: sermon preparation — which is not merely intellectual work but emotional and spiritual labor, the work of wrestling with a text until it yields something true enough to say out loud. Friday: wedding rehearsal, phone call from a church member whose child was just diagnosed with cancer. Saturday: the wedding, followed by a few hours of exhausted presence at home before the cycle begins again on Sunday.</p>

<p>By the time the pastor arrives at the dinner table — if they arrive at the dinner table — they have given away the best of themselves to people who are not their family. The empathy has been spent. The emotional availability has been drained. The capacity to be present, to listen, to engage, to care — the very capacities that the marriage needs most — have been distributed across thirty or forty pastoral interactions, and there is nothing left.</p>

<p>The spouse sees the exhaustion. The spouse understands the demands. The spouse tries to be gracious, patient, supportive. But grace has limits, patience has a shelf life, and support without reciprocity eventually becomes resentment. And the resentment, when it arrives, carries an additional burden of guilt, because the spouse knows they are resenting "God's work," and what kind of person resents God's work?</p>

<p>The kind of person who has been neglected by their spouse for fifteen years while the congregation got the best of them. That kind of person.</p>

<h2>The Fishbowl Effect</h2>

<p>Ministry marriages carry an additional burden that few other marriages face: they are lived in public. The pastor's marriage is the congregation's property — not legally, but functionally. The congregation watches how the pastor and spouse interact on Sunday mornings. They form opinions about the health of the marriage. They gossip when the spouse seems withdrawn. They expect the marriage to model what the pastor preaches, and when it does not — when the strain shows, when the facade slips, when the marriage looks ordinary rather than exemplary — the judgment arrives.</p>

<p>This public scrutiny makes vulnerability impossible. The pastor cannot stand before the congregation and say, "My marriage is struggling this month." The spouse cannot ask for prayer in the small group without it becoming a talking point in the parking lot. The couple lives inside a performance that they cannot exit, and the performance itself becomes one of the primary sources of marital stress. They are performing intimacy for an audience while the actual intimacy erodes behind the curtain.</p>

<p>The loneliest couple in most churches is the couple in the parsonage. They have access to everyone and intimacy with no one. They know the congregation's secrets but cannot share their own. They carry the weight of the church's dysfunction while pretending their own household is functional. And the pretense, sustained over years, becomes a kind of spiritual sickness — a disease of the soul that masquerades as ministerial faithfulness.</p>

<h2>The Theological Distortion</h2>

<p>Underneath the structural and emotional problems lies a theological distortion that the church has rarely examined: the idolatry of ministry. The belief — absorbed, not articulated — that pastoral work is the highest calling, that the church's needs are God's needs, and that the family must be sacrificed on the altar of vocational faithfulness.</p>

<p>This is not what Scripture teaches. Scripture teaches that the first institution God created was not the church but the family. Before there was a tabernacle, before there was a temple, before there was a congregation, there was a man and a woman in a garden, called to be one flesh. The family precedes the church in the order of creation, and while I do not want to build an entire theology on chronological priority, the ordering is not accidental. The God who made marriage before He made Israel is a God who does not regard the family as a resource to be consumed by institutional religion.</p>

<p>Jesus Himself modeled something the church has largely ignored: the discipline of withdrawal. He withdrew from the crowds to pray. He withdrew from the demands of ministry to be alone with His Father. He told His disciples, after a period of intense ministry, "Come away by yourselves to a desolate place and rest a while" (Mark 6:31). Jesus did not treat every need as a claim on His time. He did not respond to every urgency with immediate availability. He withdrew. He rested. He said no.</p>

<p>The pastor who cannot say no to the congregation for the sake of the marriage has made the congregation an idol. This is an uncomfortable sentence, and I am aware of it. But it is true. The God who called you to ministry is the same God who gave you a spouse, and He did not give you the second to sacrifice for the first.</p>

<h2>What Must Change</h2>

<p>The marriage that survives ministry does not survive by accident. It survives by deliberate, sustained, counter-cultural choices that will cost the pastor something — and the something it costs may be the approval of the congregation that has come to expect unlimited access to their pastor's life.</p>

<p>First, the pastor must establish and enforce boundaries that protect the marriage. This means designated times that are non-negotiable — date nights that do not get cancelled for committee meetings, vacations that do not include a laptop and a sermon outline, evenings that are given to the family without the phone on the table. The congregation will not like this. Some will interpret it as a lack of dedication. The pastor must be willing to pay that price, because the alternative — the slow erosion of the marriage in the name of ministerial availability — is a price that is too high.</p>

<p>Second, the pastor must find safe spaces for their own vulnerability. This means a counselor, a spiritual director, a small circle of trusted friends outside the congregation who can hold what the pastor cannot hold alone. The pastor who tries to be the sole emotional caregiver for a congregation while receiving no care themselves is a pastor who will either burn out, blow up, or both. And when the pastor burns out or blows up, the marriage is usually the first casualty.</p>

<p>Third, the congregation must change its expectations. This is the hardest shift, because it requires the congregation to see the pastor as a human being rather than a spiritual utility. The pastor is not a vending machine that dispenses pastoral care on demand. The pastor is a person with a body that gets tired, a heart that gets wounded, and a marriage that needs the same investment that every marriage needs. Congregations that demand unlimited access to their pastor are not being faithful. They are being consumptive. And the consumption will eventually destroy the person they claim to need.</p>

<blockquote>The marriage that survives ministry is not the marriage that sacrifices everything for the church. It is the marriage that recognizes that the church does not own the pastor, that the family is not an obstacle to ministry but the first ministry, and that the God who called the pastor to serve is the same God who commanded the pastor to love — and the love starts at home.</blockquote>

<p>I have learned this slowly, imperfectly, with more failure than I want to admit. My marriage has survived ministry not because I got this right but because grace covered the years when I got it wrong. That grace does not excuse the wrong. It redeems it. And the redemption calls me not to repeat the pattern but to break it — to be the pastor who goes home, who puts the phone down, who looks his wife in the eye and says, "You are not second. You were never supposed to be second. And I am sorry for every season when I made you feel like you were."</p>

<p>The ministry will survive your absence for an evening. Your marriage may not survive your absence for a decade. Choose accordingly.</p>`
